//? Title: Importance Weighted Autoencoders: Sampled Ratios and Common Selection
//? Subtitle: Matched K1/K5 Gaussian models, stable log-mean-exp and effective sample size
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An importance weighted autoencoder combines several posterior samples through the logarithm
of their mean importance weight. This report makes the sampled density ratio and ordinary
pathwise gradients explicit, then fits matched K1/K5 Gaussian models on a tiny known-factor
binary-image lattice. Both receive the same initial weights, minibatch order and first draw,
21 Adam updates and a common fixed K20 validation criterion. Both select epoch 3. Heldout
K20 negative bounds are 201.517/195.133 nats per image, while decode(mu) MSE is
0.06959/0.06612 versus 0.04855 for the fitting-only pixel mean. Weight concentration, unfiltered
reconstructions and prior probabilities retain the limitations of these short runs. A finite
Monte Carlo bound is not an exact likelihood, and no particular nested draw must improve with K.
)
\toc

> Several posterior draws, one per-image bound

[Burda, Grosse and Salakhutdinov's Importance Weighted Autoencoders](https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.00519)
use the same encoder/decoder architecture as a VAE while changing the variational objective.
For each image x, draw K independent epsilon values and set z_k=mu+exp(l/2)⊙epsilon_k.
Here q is a four-coordinate diagonal Gaussian, p(z) is standard normal, and p(x|z) is a
factorized Bernoulli decoder. The encoder is 784→32 ReLU→two four-coordinate heads;
the decoder is 4→32 ReLU→784 logits. Ten trainable tensors contain 51,416 scalars.

$$
\ell_k=\log p(z_k)-\operatorname{NLL}(x,z_k)-\log q(z_k|x),\quad
\widehat L_K(x)=\log\left(\frac1K\sum_{k=1}^K e^{\ell_k}\right),\quad
\mathcal J=-\frac1B\sum_{b=1}^B\widehat L_K(x_b).
$$
\alttext(The sampled log importance weight combines the prior, summed pixel likelihood and posterior density. Log-mean-exp reduces samples before the final average over images.)

Pixel NLL sums all 784 features; Gaussian log densities sum all four coordinates. There is no
extra division by K after log-mean-exp and no analytic KL substitution. K1 is a sampled
negative ELBO, rather than the analytic-KL estimator used by the beta-VAE report. Their
expectations agree, but their values and gradients at a supplied noise draw can differ.
All q parameters and z remain live: the implementation uses ordinary full pathwise gradients,
not a detached-weight surrogate or a doubly reparameterized gradient estimator.

